     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. I can't verify the specialist's recommendation or the chart note. The dose is also about twice the maximum, and it carries a real risk of serious harm. The risks are lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity. Metformin also gives no added glycemic benefit above about 2,000\u20132,550 mg/day, so a specialist recommending 5,100 mg/day would be unusual.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter the maximum labeled dose now**, which is 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day). Tell me if you want that.\n- **Enter a different dose** within labeled limits.\n- **Have the order go through pharmacy review.** If the dose is truly intended, the specialist's recommendation can be documented and pharmacy can verify it. Then it can be entered through your hospital's high-dose override process.\n\nIf standard dosing has failed, the specialist may have meant a different fix. Options include adding a second agent such as an SGLT2 inhibitor, a GLP-1 agonist, or insulin, or switching formulations. It would be worth confirming with them what they meant."
      }
     ],
